%  \item Bank-specific Variables:
%  \begin{itemize}
%  
%  \item  The bank size measures by its total assets [log(BankSize\_tran)] shows a negative coefficient and very significant for  most  approaches. It presents evidence that largest banks in terms of assests tend to  have  a better performance in terms of non-perfoming loans. Return on Regulatory (Risk-Adjusted) Capital [RORAC] also is significant and negative.  These results are in line with   other studies in  the empirical literature.
%  
%  \item  In this model, CET1 and leverage ratios did not show significance in most models as well as  disparities in the expected sign once macroeconomic variables are included into the model. Once certain macroeconomic indicators are excluded from the regression, these variables recover significance,specially the inclusion of the unemployment rates into the models seems to strongly impact the coefficient for CET1.Once unemployment rares are excluded from the regression, CET1 and leverage ratios show a negative and significant impact on NPL ratios as suggested in other empirical studies.
%  
%  \item Net interest margins [NIM\_tr] are strongly positive and significant as expected under (non-dynamic) standard models but once bank individual effects are control for these effects disappeared. Cost to income shows  a negative coefficient with moderate impact and significance  in a fixed effects approach. Cost-to-income is in this context also interesting because part of the collection costs  for non-performing loans may  also be included in the administrative expenses. 
%  
%  \item The dummy  regarding membership  in the  Single Supervisory Mechanism [SSM] shows negative and very significant coeffcients for all models considered, suggesting negative impact on non-performing loans of SSM membership. Nevertheless, the results here are to be taken with caution due to the assymetric distribution between SSM members and non-members in the sample.
%  
%  \item The Cross-Border Diversification Index [CBDI index] has a positive and statistically significant  coefficient in most panel data approaches, although the total impacts seems to be moderate, suggesting than banks with more geographic diversification in their portfolios tend to show slightly higher NPL ratios at consolidated level. SSM membership also shows a negative correlation with NPLs ratios as well as significance in most approaches.
%   \end{itemize}
%   
% \item Macroeconomic Indicators:
% 
% \begin{itemize}
% 
% \item Institutional  variables  are the strength of legal rights [Stgth\_legal\_index] and the Recovery rate [Reco\_rate]  at country level. The Recovery rate [Reco\_rate]  at country level show  a negative and very significant correlation with NPL ratios in most approaches, suggesting certain theoretical implications that the effectivity  in the recovery of debt have a positiv impact on reducing NPL ratios. Interestingly, given the data a  positive but low impact  of the strengh of the legal sistem on NPLS is detected, although not significant in a dynamic approach. This may imply that countries providing better laws to protect borrowers and lenders also tend to have showed higher NPLs as well. In general, these effects needs a deeper look.
% 
% \item Gross public debt and unemployment rates has a positive an significant impact on NPL ratios as also  concluded in  the empirical literature. Inflation rates are strongly negative and very significant. These results confirms the importance of these standard macroeconomic  indiciators in explaining non-perfoming loans.
% 
% \item  The current account balance [curr\_acc\_bal] shows a  slightly positive and statistically significant relationship with NPL ratios in most approaches. In past years, the relationship between external deficits and  non-performing loans has been of special interest in the context of  the consequences of current account imbalances. In this context, the positive correlation obtained in this sample and period is due to the measures taken by countries as Spain, Greece and Cyprus as part of the long-run economic measures to improve the balance of payments, which tradionally also tend to have higher NPLs as the european average. In a dynamic approach, the current account loses significance. In general, a deeper look here  is required.
% 
% \item The annual GDP growth as percent of GDP also show  slightly positive coefficients and significance. Although these results here are somehow counterintuitive, there is some similar results also  available in the empirical literature that find a positive relationship. This point also requires further analysis.
% 
% 
%  \end{itemize}
%  
%  
%  \end{itemize}
