%  \subsection{ Quantile regression Models}
%  
%   %\input{Tables/Final/quantile/Quantil_Regression_npl_hh.nfi_pr}
%   %\input{Tables/Final/quantile/Slope_Test_a}
%   
%    % \input{Tables/Final/quantile/Quantil_Regression_npl_hh}
%     %\input{Tables/Final/quantile/Quantil_Regression_npl_nfi}
%     % \input{Tables/Final/quantile/Quantil_Regression_npl_fi}
%      
%    Tables \ref{qq.npl_hh},\ref{qq.npl_nfi} and \ref{qq.npl_fi} show the results for the quantile regressions. Figures \ref{qq.graph.hh},\ref{qq.graph.nfi} and \ref{qq.graph.fi} shows the  results from the quantile regression estimation in a graphical form.
%   % \ref{qq.npl_hh.nfi}
%    
%   As explained previously, the use of quantile regression in this context offers a interesting approach for the following reasons:
%   \begin{itemize}
%  \item Additional robustness checks
%  \item Relaxation of distribution assumptions. NPL ratios show a non-normal distribution with negative skewness(left skewed distribution)
%  \item Analysis of impact on NPL at different points of its quantile distribution. The sample show high heterogeneity along the NPL quantile distribution.
%  \end{itemize}
%  
% 
% The preliminary results of the quantile regression estimation regarding households and non-financial corporations are \textit{(to be further develop in next drafts)}:
%  \begin{itemize}
%  \item Bank-specific Variables
%  \begin{itemize}
%  \item Net interest margins are very significant and positive correlated with increasing effects along the distribution. Return on Regulatiory Capita [RORAC] also show very significant and  large negative coefficients.
%  
%  \item Leverate  and capital ratios [LEVER\_tr\_ratio,CET1\_ratio\_tr] show in general not much statistical significance. In the case of leverage ratios, significant increase for the higher quantiles with positive sign whereas CET1 ratios remains with negative coefficient in the lower quantiles tending  to zero along the conditional distribution. Cost to income seems to be widely insignificant and low except for higher quantiles cases where the effects are increasingly negative.
%  
%   \item The Cross-Border Diversification Index [CBDI\_index]  shows a positive relation with NPLs, increasing along the distribution with significance at the highest quantiles.  In this approach,the SSM dummy for membership in the Single Supervisory Mechanism  also shows a strong significance and negative correlation, suggesting than SSM membership effets on NPLs increases around the highest quantiles where banks with the highest NPLs accumulate. These results are similar to the ones obtained in the above presented panel models.
%   
% 
%   \end{itemize}
%  \item Macroeconomic Indicators
%   \begin{itemize}
%   
%   \item Regarding institutional variables, the recovery rates [Reco\_rate\_dol] are negative and significant along the distribution. In regard to  the strength of the legal system [Stgth\_legal\_index], it shows in general statistically no significant coefficients along the conditional distribution with the exception of the highest quantiles where the significance increase with positive coefficients. This last result differ from the result in  the above presented panel models in the sense that the strength of the legal system seems to have an impact on NPLs for banks that present the highest NPLs. One possible explanation may indicate that many european countries, where the NPL  were historically high, ha tended to implement stronger laws regarding debt  in recent years. In general, these effects needs a deeper look.
%   
%   \item The current account balance remains significant and positive. Bank sizes shows to have a negative and a very signicant coefficients along the conditional distribution. Similar to public debt
%   
%   \item Unemployment remains a positive coefficient but loses significant along in the tails.  The estimates also remain  similar along the conditional distribution. Inflation shows a negative correlation and strong statistical significance which  impact progressively  increases along the conditional distribution.
%   
%   \end{itemize}
%   
%   \item  The sign of most coefficients as well as its significance are close to those obtained in the panel models presented above and, to some extense, confirming previous results.
%   
%   \item In general, both bank-specific variables and macroeconomic indicators with significant  and positive or negative effects seems to increase their intensity  along the quantile distribution. In other words, given the current model, it seems that the effects of macro and bank-specific variables on NPLs are even more intense  aroung the higher quantiles where banks with the highest NPLs are concentrated.
% 
%   
%  \end{itemize}
% 
%    
%    Table \ref{slope} shows the slope test for quantile regressions.
%    
% 
%     
%   The previous results are contained based on traditional quantile regression methods. quantile Regression methods for panel data with fixed effects is very actual and currently active ground for new research, and therefore,  available panel quantile regression methods are still being constantly  refined and improved. In this paper,the penalized fixed effects quantile Regression method  proposed by Koecker is considered as example of a panel quantile regression with fixed effects as explained in previous sections. This approach is commonly used  in several empirical studies covering quantile regressions in panel data, and therefore, its consideration in this paper may offer  additional robustness checks in the context of fixed effects analyses.
%   
%   As explained in previous sections, when $\lambda \to 0$ the model is similar to a (weigthed) dummy-variable quantile regression and when $\lambda \to \infty$ the model is more like a (weigthed) cross-section quantile regression. The penaly allows a "control" over the many fixed effects coefficients by not letting them reach unrealistic large numbers.
% 
%  The  calibration parameters used in this paper   for $\lambda$ are 1, 2.5 and 5. The reason for the selection of these values is based on previous empirical studies, which show that from  a $\lambda$ equal to 5, the model already is close to a (weigthed) cross-section quantile regression and with $\lambda$ equal to 1 the model is already  expected to approximate a (weigthed) dummy-variable quantile regression. 
%   
%   
%    %\input{Tables/Final/quantile/QRFE_npl_hh.nfi}
%   
%   
%    
%    %\ref{qrfe_npl_hh.nfi}
%    Table \ref{qrfe_npl_hh},\ref{qrfe_npl_nfi} and figures  \ref{feqq1.graph.hh}, \ref{feqq2.5.graph.hh} and
%    \ref{feqq5.graph.hh} ,\ref{feqq1.graph.nfi}, \ref{feqq2.5.graph.nfi} and
%    \ref{feqq5.graph.nfi} shows  the results of a fixed effects quantile regression estimation for households  and non-financial corporations with $\lambda=1, 2.5, 5$.
%    
%    Table \ref{qrfe_npl_fi} and figures  \ref{feqq1.graph.fi}, \ref{feqq2.5.graph.fi} and
%    \ref{feqq5.graph.fi} shows  the results of a fixed effects quantile regression estimation for financial corporations  with $\lambda=1, 2.5, 5$.
%    
%   The preliminary results of the fixed effects quantile regression estimation for $\lambda= 1, 2.5, 5$ regarding households and non-financial corporations are \textit{(to be further develop in next drafts)}:
%   
%   
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Bank-specific Variables
% 
%   \begin{itemize}
%   \item  Net Interest Margin [NIM\_tr] is significant and positive along the conditional distribution. Its coefficient is also large and increase progressively for higher  quantiles. This result is in line with  the ones obtained by the previous models. Returns on Regulatory Capital [RORAC] are less significant when higher fixed effects are considered ($\lambda= 1$)  but also with large negative coefficients, which impacts increase along the conditional distribution for all $\lambda$.
%   
%   
%   \item The Cross-Border Diversification Index is relatively large and positive for  all $\lambda$ and very significant for the cases where strong fixed effects are assumed ($\lambda= 1$).
%   
%   \item The impact of dummy variable for membership in the Single Supervisory Mechanism(SSM) on NPLs is significant and negative. Once  higher fixed effects are considered this impact remains constant along the conditional distribution of NPLs (a flat curve)
%  
%   \item The results for the rest of bank-specific variables are similar to the ones obtained in previous models.
%   
%   
%   \end{itemize}
%   
% \item Macroeconomic Indicators
% 
%   \begin{itemize}
%   
%   \item Unemployment rates [unempl\_rate\_imf] show a large positive and very significant coefficients once higher fixed effects are considered ($\lambda= 1$). Its significant decrease for $\lambda= 1$ and $\lambda= 2$. Interestingly, the estimated impact of the unemployment rates remains constant along the conditional distribution of NPLs (a flat curve).
%   
%   \item Recovery rates [Reco\_rate\_dol] are very significant and negative for all $\lambda$ . Its impact along the conditional distribution of NPLs is  slighly increasing for  higher quantiles. The streng of the legal system [Stgth\_legal\_index] is positive but insignificant for all $\lambda$.
%   
%   \item Inflation rates [infl\_perc] are negative  with an increasing impact along the conditional distribution of NPLs. Interestingly, its significant considerably decrease for middle and lower quantiles when  higher fixed effects are considered ($\lambda= 1)
%   
%   \item The results for the rest of macreconomic indicators are similar to the ones obtained in previous models.
%   
%   \end{itemize}
%   
% 
% \end{itemize}
% 
% 
% \textit{In general, a futher analysis of the empirical results,including its intermodel comparison and  relative importance, will be provided once the different tests for model adequacy (heterogeneity, correlated effects, serial correlation ect..) are taken into consideration. Most important determinants should then be identified and filtered out for conclusions.}
%   
%\end {enumerate}

